-- A refined annotation on a LOCAL `let` is a CHECKED obligation, and a TRUE
-- one still composes into a later call.
--
-- `ys` is annotated `{List(Int) | len(_) > 0}` and bound to `[1]`, which
-- satisfies it. Two obligations are discharged here, not one: the annotation
-- itself is proved against the expression it annotates, and the proved fact
-- then discharges `inner(ys)`'s identical precondition through the
-- contract-composition machinery of
-- `accept/t128_refine_list_contract_composes_call`.
--
-- Before 2026-07-30 only the SECOND of those happened. `scope_add_binding`
-- admitted an annotated `let`'s predicate into the refined scope channel
-- unconditionally -- `A.PatVar n, Some r -> (n.A.txt, r) :: sc` -- so the
-- annotation was a promise the checker simply believed. This file passed then
-- too, for the wrong reason, which is exactly why an accept-only witness
-- cannot pin this behaviour: a trusted annotation and a verified one both
-- exit 0. The discriminating case is its bracket,
-- `reject/t131_refine_let_annotation_false`, where the annotated expression
-- plainly violates the annotation and the old checker PROVED the downstream
-- call off it anyway.
--
-- The counted version of this file's claim lives in the `let-annotation`
-- suite in `test/test_refinecheck.ml`, which asserts 2 proved / 0 violated /
-- 0 skipped -- the "2" being the assertion with teeth, since the pre-fix
-- behaviour also produced a passing exit code with 1.
--
-- See specs/lang/refinement-types.md.
mod T130 do

  fn inner(xs : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0}) : Int do
    0
  end

  fn outer() : Int do
    let ys : {List(Int) | len(_) > 0} = [1]
    inner(ys)
  end

  fn main() : Int do
    outer()
  end

end
